  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reference value is zero, since there is no sure outcome (i.e., no outcome with 100% probability) in either lottery. The differences between each outcome and the reference value (zero) are simply the outcomes themselves: for Lottery A, the differences are -6000 (with 0.1%) and 0 (with 99.9%); for Lottery B, -3000 (with 0.2%) and 0 (with 99.8%). The mean difference for each lottery is calculated as the probability-weighted sum of these differences: for A, mean difference = (0.001 × -6000) + (0.999 × 0) = -6; for B, mean difference = (0.002 × -3000) + (0.998 × 0) = -6. Both lotteries have the same mean difference (-6), so the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
